Bohr kept Rutherford's small positive nucleus with an orbiting electron, and added postulates:
- the electron can occupy only certain stable orbits (stationary states) with quantised energy, in which it does not radiate;
- these orbits have quantised angular momentum, $mvr=\dfrac{nh}{2\pi}$;
- radiation is emitted or absorbed only when the electron jumps between states, with photon energy $hf=|E_i-E_f|$.

**Emission lines:** an excited electron falls to a lower level and emits a photon with exactly the energy difference. Only certain differences exist, so only certain wavelengths appear as bright lines.

**Absorption lines:** an electron absorbs a photon only if its energy exactly matches a difference between levels, jumping to a higher level. Those wavelengths are removed from a continuous spectrum, leaving dark lines.

Three forces act on the car:

- its **weight** $F_w=mg$, vertically downward;
- the **normal force** $N$ from the track, perpendicular to the surface of the track (tilted $30^\circ$ from the vertical, towards the centre of the circle);
- the **force of friction** $F_{\text{fric}}$, acting down the slope along the surface (since the car is at its *maximum* speed, it is on the verge of sliding up and outwards, so friction acts down the incline towards the centre).

The **net force** is the centripetal force $F_c$, which is **horizontal**, directed towards the centre of the circular path.

questions.question:
A circular wire loop is placed near a long straight wire carrying a constant current in the direction shown. The loop moves three times:

A – it rotates once, uniformly about the Y-axis with the resistor R moving out of the page initially;

B – it moves parallel to the straight wire with constant speed;

C – it moves away perpendicularly from the straight wire with constant speed.

Complete the table in terms of the motions of A, B and C by sketching the emf induced in the loop and state whether the direction of the emf is clockwise, anticlockwise or not relevant.

